# Bike Parts Management — agent rules

## Product

- Audience: Bangladesh motorcycle parts shop owners (non-technical). UI Bangla-first.
- Multi-tenant: one email login → one shop; RLS isolates shop data. Shared parts catalog.
- Auth: email OTP only for free path. No paid SMS/Twilio. No paid email SaaS required (Gmail App Password SMTP OK).

## Hard constraints (free)

- Do not introduce paid APIs, paid SMTP products, paid analytics, or paid Cursor/Cloud features as requirements.
- Prefer: Vite + React + Supabase free tier + Netlify free + browser APIs.
- Never commit `.env`, service_role keys, or Gmail app passwords.
- Never put developer setup (SQL/SMTP/App password steps) on shop-owner screens. Setup only when schema/auth is broken.

## Agentic bookkeeping (required)

After any meaningful change in a session, update `docs/STATUS.md`:

1. Move items between Done / In progress / Next / Gaps.
2. Note date and one-line what changed.
3. If a phase finishes, tick it in `docs/ROADMAP.md`.

Before starting large work, read `docs/STATUS.md` and `docs/ROADMAP.md` so you do not redo finished work or skip gaps.

## UX direction

- Desktop (≥1024px): sidebar + analytical dashboard + split panels for sale/purchase.
- Phone: bottom nav + large taps + scan/search first.
- Keep workshop green/orange + Anek Bangla / Noto Sans Bengali. No purple SaaS / cream-serif / broadsheet defaults.

## Code habits

- Prefer editing existing modules (`src/lib/data.tsx`, `cloud.ts`, pages) over new parallel systems.
- Match existing patterns; no drive-by refactors.
- Do not push to git or create commits unless the user asks.
